  values ('ME', 'short_answer', '''Out of pocket expenditure'' was 16% for Bhutan in 2018. This is interesting given that health care is free in Bhutan. Briefly describe 3 possible factors contributing to out-of-pocket expenditure. (3 marks)', '(1) Indirect costs: although treatment is free, patients and their attendants pay for travel, food and lodging when they go to district or referral hospitals or are referred to Thimphu, and they lose wages while away. (2) Buying medicines and services privately: when medicines, supplies or tests are out of stock or not on the essential list, people buy them from private pharmacies and diagnostic centres; they also pay for spectacles, some dental care and over-the-counter drugs. (3) Seeking care outside the free system: some people go to private clinics or travel to India or elsewhere for treatment on their own (self-referral), and many spend on rituals and traditional healing; these all add to out-of-pocket spending.', null, '[{"point": "Indirect costs of seeking care: travel, food and lodging for patients and attendants, especially for referrals to Thimphu or abroad; lost wages", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Private purchase: medicines/ supplies not available or out of stock, private clinics, diagnostic services, pharmacies, spectacles, dental items", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Treatment outside the free system: self-referral and private treatment in India/ abroad, traditional/ religious healing (rituals), patient preference for private services", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
    (select t.id from public.topics t where t.subject_id = (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge') and t.slug = 'medical-science-general-subject-knowledge'), (select id from public.exam_papers where code = 'me-technical-2'), 'standard', 3,
