  values ('ME', 'short_answer', 'Define diffusion and facilitated diffusion. List one primary difference between the two. (2+1 marks)', 'Diffusion is the net movement of molecules or ions from a region of higher concentration to a region of lower concentration until they are evenly spread; it needs no energy and small, non-polar molecules such as oxygen and carbon dioxide diffuse directly through the lipid bilayer. Facilitated diffusion is also passive movement down the concentration gradient without ATP, but it takes place with the help of specific carrier or channel proteins in the membrane, for substances that cannot cross the lipid layer easily, such as glucose and ions. Primary difference: simple diffusion does not need membrane proteins, whereas facilitated diffusion depends on specific transport proteins, so it is selective and reaches a maximum rate when all carriers are busy (saturation).', null, '[{"point": "Diffusion: net movement of molecules from a region of higher concentration to lower concentration (down the gradient) without energy, directly through the membrane (e.g. O2, CO2)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Facilitated diffusion: passive movement down the concentration gradient with the help of specific carrier or channel proteins in the membrane, no ATP (e.g. glucose into red cells, ions through channels)", "marks": 1}, {"point": "Difference: simple diffusion needs no membrane protein; facilitated diffusion needs specific carrier/ channel proteins (so it is specific and can be saturated)", "marks": 1}]'::jsonb, (select id from public.subjects where slug = 'general-subject-knowledge'),
